course: dictionary
lesson: 452
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is needed. The word argument can mean an angry quarrel, or a reason given to show that something is right. «You do not have to win every argument, even when you are right.» Here an argument is a quarrel between people who disagree.
explain: «A clear family rule about buying, such as one purchase a month, prevents most arguments.» Ask: what would fit here? A family rule stops quarrels, so argument means a quarrel. Now try the other meaning in the sentence, a reason, and see if it still makes sense. It does not, so you know which meaning is right.
explain: In school work, argument often means a reason or set of reasons. A good argument gives reasons and evidence to show your opinion is right. When you read or write, check the words around the word. Angry words, shouting or disagreement point to a quarrel. Words like evidence, because or proof point to a reason.
fact: An argument can be an angry quarrel between people who disagree.
fact: An argument can also be a reason, or set of reasons, that shows something is right or true.
fact: «Row» is a British word for a loud argument.
fact: Useful phrases: have an argument, win an argument, an argument for, an argument against.
fact: Check the context: if your meaning does not fit the sentence, try the other meaning.
exercise: «You do not have to win every argument, even when you are right.» What does argument mean here? | A type of rain | A piece of maths homework | *An angry quarrel between people who disagree | A long car journey
exercise: Maya says, «My argument is that we should have a longer break, because we work hard all morning.» What does argument mean? | A loud shouting match in the playground | A friendly game of tag | *A reason that supports her idea | A shopping list
exercise: What does «row» mean in British English? | A line of seats in a theatre | A type of garden flower | *A loud argument | A small boat
test: «Lending money to friends can cause arguments, so many grown-ups only lend what they could afford to lose.» What causes the arguments? | Grown-ups | Afford | Losing | *Lending money to friends
test: What does the phrase «an argument for» mean? | A question with no answer | *A reason that supports something | A loud noise made by a crowd | A quarrel against someone
exam: Read this: «Our teacher said my argument was clear, with good reasons and evidence.» Which meaning is used? | An angry quarrel between people | *A reason given to show something is right | Loud shouting in a classroom | A friendly chat about school
